#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087523 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9ec9e0af-bbdc-5a29-bdbb-602e4d1f016a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Acceleration Vector of a Particle Given Its Velocity Vector"}] :question/id #uuid "e052cb8a-7337-57ef-99b4-65a1c742c4bf" :question/math-academy-id "e-620" :question/problem "The velocity vector $\\mathbf{v} = \\langle u, v \\rangle$ of a particle $P$ at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v} = \\langle 12t+t^2, t^3+1\\rangle.\n$$\n\nCalculate the acceleration vector $\\mathbf{a}$." :question/worked-solution "To compute the acceleration vector, we differentiate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(12t+{t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}+1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨12+2t,\\,3{t}^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087524 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "380357c3-4914-5137-9449-0e02076cb248" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Acceleration Vector of a Particle Given Its Position Vector"}] :question/id #uuid "88214928-ff75-51ac-bb97-f04b1f45360b" :question/math-academy-id "e-621" :question/problem "The position vector $\\mathbf{r}$ of a particle $P$ (relative to a fixed origin $O$) at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = \\langle 4t^2+1, 3t^2-t\\rangle.\n$$\n\nCalculate the acceleration vector $\\mathbf{a}$." :question/worked-solution "To compute the acceleration vector, we need to differentiate the velocity vector. So first, we need to compute the velocity vector. We can do this by differentiating the position vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4{t}^{2}+1),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{2}-t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨8t,\\,6t-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to compute the acceleration vector, we differentiate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(8t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6t-1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨8,6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087559 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "318d38c5-d3fb-52bf-8a88-5592f8301f89" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Acceleration of a Particle at a Given Moment Given Its Velocity Vector"}] :question/id #uuid "c01d6202-76ac-5a5c-850f-5b699d01c462" :question/math-academy-id "e-7224" :question/problem "The velocity vector $\\mathbf{v} = \\langle u, v \\rangle,$ measured in $\\textrm{m}/\\textrm s,$ of a particle $P$ at time $t$ seconds, is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v} = \\langle t^4-t^2, 2t^3\\rangle.\n$$\n\nFind the acceleration vector and the magnitude of the acceleration of $P$ at time $t=1.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the acceleration vector, we differentiate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{4}-{t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{3})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4{t}^{3}-2t,\\,6{t}^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the acceleration at time $t=1,$ we substitute $t=1$ into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(1) & =⟨4(1)^{3}-2(1),6(1)^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the magnitude of the acceleration, we compute $|\\mathbf a(1)|\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|a(1)| & =|⟨2,6⟩| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{6}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{40} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{10}\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087560 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "17408599-c601-5c8c-96a7-da7566ba07c1" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Acceleration of a Particle at a Given Moment Given Its Position Vector"}] :question/id #uuid "b7bffc21-d43c-5b2b-b09b-649d17b1eb96" :question/math-academy-id "e-7225" :question/problem "The position vector $\\mathbf{r},$ in meters, of a particle $P$ relative to a fixed origin $O$ at time $t$ seconds is given by\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = \\left\\langle t^{5}-t^2,\\, t^2+6t-3 \\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\nCalculate the acceleration vector and the magnitude of the acceleration of $P$ at time $t=0.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the acceleration vector, we need to differentiate the velocity vector. So first, we need to compute the velocity vector, which we can do this by differentiating the position vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{5}-{t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}+6t-3)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5{t}^{4}-2t,\\,2t+6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to compute the acceleration vector, we differentiate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5{t}^{4}-2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t+6)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨20{t}^{3}-2,\\,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the acceleration at time $t=0,$ we substitute $t=0$ into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(0) & =⟨20(0)^{3}-2,\\,2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2,\\,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the magnitude of the acceleration, we compute $|\\mathbf a(0)|\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|a(0)| & =|⟨-2,\\,2⟩| \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{8} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309604 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "318d38c5-d3fb-52bf-8a88-5592f8301f89" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Acceleration of a Particle at a Given Moment Given Its Velocity Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4085bd9e-1247-5666-8815-aac532533d78" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a(2)=⟨\\frac{3}{7},-2⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309606} {:answer/id #uuid "ff3ff216-38aa-57e4-b23f-625078696242" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a(2)=⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡3,-7⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309607} {:answer/id #uuid "cecbd97b-7628-50d6-95f6-e7c18e8b9b52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a(2)=⟨\\frac{3}{5},-7⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309608} {:answer/id #uuid "07f65406-08e2-5687-9e82-865caec479cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a(2)=⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡7,-4⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309609} {:answer/id #uuid "47d791e7-d07f-50cf-9bd9-a2151fcf8d50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a(2)=⟨\\frac{3}{7},-4⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309610}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "47d791e7-d07f-50cf-9bd9-a2151fcf8d50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a(2)=⟨\\frac{3}{7},-4⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309610} :answer-field/id #uuid "c9729a40-615c-546c-828f-575525d28002" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309605}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "485b83d3-77da-50d6-9d88-1eeec6210db1" :question/math-academy-id "q-121919" :question/problem "The velocity vector $v=⟨u,v⟩$ of a particle $P$ at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\nv=⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3t+1),1-{t}^{2}⟩,\\,t>0.\n$$\n\nCalculate the acceleration vector $a$ at time $t=2.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the acceleration vector, we differentiate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t},\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3t+1)),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-{t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{3}{3t+1},-2t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the acceleration at time $t=2,$ we substitute $t=2$ into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(2) & =⟨\\frac{3}{3(2)+1},-2(2)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{3}{7},-4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088078 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "17408599-c601-5c8c-96a7-da7566ba07c1" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Acceleration of a Particle at a Given Moment Given Its Position Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8181d51f-9890-5c71-8981-ac5153b3f13d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{5}m/s^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085374} {:answer/id #uuid "9880d8b3-2f4a-5722-b72c-c164d1a02ba5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}m/s^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085375} {:answer/id #uuid "4557035c-8e63-5646-a11e-3425321b822c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\sqrt{3}m/s^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085376} {:answer/id #uuid "5c80616d-9a66-5171-ba4a-83504e0b3a0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{5}m/s^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085377} {:answer/id #uuid "72249dfd-1354-5287-a901-8d1e579489bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\sqrt{5}m/s^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085378}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "72249dfd-1354-5287-a901-8d1e579489bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\sqrt{5}m/s^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085378} :answer-field/id #uuid "ae66fc2a-1f63-55e7-ace7-c1693a30e3d2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085373}] :question/id #uuid "faa1910e-2f93-51e3-bcbb-cd2ceaa6a0a3" :question/math-academy-id "q-31683" :question/problem "The position vector $r$, in meters, of a particle $P$ relative to a fixed origin $O$ at time $t$ seconds is given by\n\n$r = ⟨4t^{3/2} + 4t^{2} + 5, t^{3} - 4t^{2} + t⟩, t > 0$.\nCalculate the magnitude of the acceleration of $P$ at time $t = 1$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186309637 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9ec9e0af-bbdc-5a29-bdbb-602e4d1f016a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Acceleration Vector of a Particle Given Its Velocity Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "62e8efa6-4972-50f9-b498-6c7524dd3c25" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3e^{3t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309639} {:answer/id #uuid "392a91b4-debd-575a-8ac2-0880734f81ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{3t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309640}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "62e8efa6-4972-50f9-b498-6c7524dd3c25" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3e^{3t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309639} :answer-field/id #uuid "43e493cd-5784-560a-899b-78f9ddc307aa" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309638} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "db5950e6-0ebf-59a6-a609-65f4075687e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3t^2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309642}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "db5950e6-0ebf-59a6-a609-65f4075687e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3t^2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309642} :answer-field/id #uuid "57298f26-63eb-5cb0-b7bb-93bee04ed24c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186309641}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c8d865e3-b227-5cb9-adc4-9f5a7bac4f46" :question/math-academy-id "q-351111" :question/problem "The velocity vector $v=⟨u,v⟩$ of a particle $P$ at time $t$ is given by\n\n$$\nv=⟨{e}^{3t},\\,{t}^{3}⟩.\n$$\n\nCalculate the components of the acceleration vector $a$ at time $t.$\n\n$a=⟨${{field-1}},{{field-2}}$⟩$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the acceleration vector, we differentiate the velocity vector:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{3t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3{e}^{3t},\\,3{t}^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186088164 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "318d38c5-d3fb-52bf-8a88-5592f8301f89" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Acceleration of a Particle at a Given Moment Given Its Velocity Vector"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a2d1abb8-d277-59cd-834f-b446a7039059" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a(3) = ⟨27, 3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085916} {:answer/id #uuid "bc66a20c-61c4-5d2b-81fc-69c63c103ec1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a(3) = ⟨9,-3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085917} {:answer/id #uuid "1734c43c-d420-567e-915d-1e64154713c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a(3) = ⟨27,-3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085918} {:answer/id #uuid "2c276e51-9d9b-542a-acfe-92f863b7aba6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a(3) = ⟨81, 3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085919} {:answer/id #uuid "7137b11f-3a9f-58c2-b41e-1e963b0efe8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a(3) = ⟨81,-3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085920}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7137b11f-3a9f-58c2-b41e-1e963b0efe8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "a(3) = ⟨81,-3⟩" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085920} :answer-field/id #uuid "611eff44-97e1-5e18-90a6-337ee45922bb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186085915}] :question/id #uuid "0f32a2ee-c5f0-5f24-b5be-ed835365541a" :question/math-academy-id "q-6392" :question/problem "The velocity vector $